From: CARGT <engineering@cargt.com>
Subject: [PATCH] resize-helper: only grow userfs, not the fixed-size A/B
 slots

bootfs, vendorfs and rootfs are duplicated into -a/-b RAUC OTA slots,
each built to (and flashed at) a fixed size with no "remaining space"
to grow into - unlike userfs, which is intentionally built small and
grown once to fill whatever's left on the eMMC. Both the systemd-growfs
path and the direct-resize2fs fallback here unconditionally process
every mounted ext4 filesystem (/, /boot, /vendor, /usr/local),
including a full e2fsck -c bad-blocks scan in the fallback path - on
every first boot of a freshly-built rootfs. For RAUC that means every
OTA update's first boot into the slot that was just updated, not just
a one-time factory cost. Restrict both paths to userfs (identified by
its unique, non-duplicated GPT partition label) since it's the only
partition here that still needs growing.

Upstream-Status: Pending [not sent upstream to ST - specific to a
custom A/B partition scheme]

--- a/resize-helper
+++ b/resize-helper
@@ -54,9 +54,6 @@
     then
         [ -f /sbin/mount-partitions.sh ] && /sbin/mount-partitions.sh stop
     fi
-    /lib/systemd/systemd-growfs /
-    /lib/systemd/systemd-growfs /boot
-    /lib/systemd/systemd-growfs /vendor
     /lib/systemd/systemd-growfs /usr/local/
 else
     echo "RESIZE-HELPER: Using directly resize2fs" > /dev/kmsg
@@ -66,9 +63,12 @@
         PART_TABLE_TYPE=$(udevadm info --query=property --name=/dev/${device} | grep '^ID_PART_TABLE_TYPE=' | cut -d'=' -f2)
         if [ -e /dev/${device} ]; then
             if [ "$PART_TABLE_TYPE" = "gpt" ]; then
-                echo "Resize /dev/${device}"
-                e2fsck -f -y -c -C 0 "/dev/${device}"
-                ${RESIZE2FS} "/dev/${device}"
+                PART_LABEL=$(udevadm info --query=property --name=/dev/${device} | grep '^ID_PART_ENTRY_NAME=' | cut -d'=' -f2)
+                if [ "$PART_LABEL" = "userfs" ]; then
+                    echo "Resize /dev/${device}"
+                    e2fsck -f -y -c -C 0 "/dev/${device}"
+                    ${RESIZE2FS} "/dev/${device}"
+                fi
             fi
         fi
     done
